{"name":"Cognac Tint","hex":"#a17b49","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cognac","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A brandy distilled from white wine in the region around Cognac in France.","A dark orangish brown color."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cognac"},{"term":"tint","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A slight coloring.","A pale or faint tinge of any color; especially, a variation of a color obtained by adding white (contrast shade)"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To shade, to color."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tint"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cognac","blocks":[{"text":"Cognac is a variety of brandy named after the commune of Cognac, France. It is produced in the surrounding departments of Charente and Charente-Maritime, in an officially designated wine-growing region. This region is divided into six districts with different cognacs produced in each."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Cognac_glass.jpg/330px-Cognac_glass.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognac"},{"title":"Tint, shade and tone","blocks":[{"text":"In color theory, a tint is a mixture of a color with white, which increases lightness, while a shade is a mixture with black, which increases darkness. A tone is produced either by mixing a color with gray, or by both tinting and shading. Mixing a color with any neutral color reduces the chroma, or colorfulness, while the perceived hue can be affected slightly."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Tint-tone-shade.svg/330px-Tint-tone-shade.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tint%2C_shade_and_tone"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Cognac_glass_-_tulip_shaped.JPG/500px-Cognac_glass_-_tulip_shaped.JPG?utm_so